*- [EPIC] [TI-0011] Phase 0 audio, model, and privacy feasibility
  [DESC] Validate the platform-sensitive assumptions before investing in the production application.

    $- [FT] [TI-0012] Build an Ubuntu audio-capture feasibility spike
      [DESC] Provide consent-gated microphone and system-audio capture as exact, recoverable PCM WAV chunks with an atomic session manifest.
      [DESC] Testing found system playback in the microphone track; source routing or acoustic bleed must be diagnosed before approval.
      [DESC] Experimental offline adaptive filtering degraded voice clarity and must not be used as the production echo-cancellation path.
      [DESC] The replacement WebRTC mode preserves raw microphone audio, produces an echo-cancelled microphone track, and restores temporary playback routing after capture.
      [DESC] Accepted for the MVP with a headset recommendation and a warning that loud simultaneous system playback can suppress local speech.
    *- [ENH] [TI-0013] Verify separate and mixed audio tracks
      [DESC] Exercise microphone-only, system-audio-only, and combined capture paths.
      [DESC] Measure source readiness, establish a common session start, and verify track alignment after device wake-up.
    *- [ENH] [TI-0014] Benchmark two English transcription model sizes
      [DESC] Measure accuracy and speed across Indian accents, background noise, and domain terminology.
    *- [ENH] [TI-0015] Benchmark experimental multilingual transcription
      [DESC] Evaluate Malayalam, Hindi, Japanese, Manglish, Hinglish, and Japanese-English code-switching without making these results a release gate.
    *- [ENH] [TI-0016] Evaluate speaker-separation options
      [DESC] Compare licensing, downloads, runtime, hardware requirements, and labeling accuracy.
    ~- [ENH] [TI-0017] Select transcription, speaker, and summary strategies
      [DESC] Base model choices and realistic processing targets on measured Phase 0 results.
    *- [ENH] [TI-0018] Publish the feasibility report and benchmark results
    ~- [ENH] [TI-0019] Confirm measurable first-release acceptance criteria
      [DESC] Cover recording reliability, processing completion, transcript usability, summary grounding, privacy, and interruption recovery.

*- [EPIC] [TI-0020] Application foundation and recoverable job architecture
  [DESC] Establish the desktop shell, persistence, automated checks, and retry-safe processing foundation.

    $- [FT] [TI-0021] Create the repository structure and coding standards
      [DESC] Configure automated checks that run on every change.
      [DESC] Establish the repository layout, contribution rules, lockfile, continuous integration, and versioned pre-commit validation.
    $- [FT] [TI-0022] Build the Tauri, React, TypeScript, and Tailwind desktop shell
      [DESC] Provide a responsive local-first workspace shell, headset guidance, automated UI tests, and native Tauri continuous-integration checks.
      [DESC] Native Ubuntu testing found missing pkg-config and D-Bus development packages; retry after installing pkg-config and libdbus-1-dev.
      [DESC] Follow-up testing confirmed the main WebKitGTK, GTK, GLib, AppIndicator, SVG, Xdo, and OpenSSL development packages were not installed.
      [DESC] APT then exposed a disabled jammy-updates repository, leaving installed Pango runtime packages newer than the visible development packages.
      [DESC] Native Ubuntu launch, locked Rust compilation, frontend tests, and the production web build passed after restoring the standard development packages.
    $- [FT] [TI-0023] Add SQLite persistence and database migrations
      [DESC] Store meetings, transcript segments, jobs, actions, and settings.
      [DESC] Initialize bundled SQLite in the per-user application-data directory and apply ordered migrations transactionally.
      [DESC] Verified the live application database, migration idempotence, referential cleanup, integrity, Rust formatting, and warning-free Clippy output.
    $- [FT] [TI-0024] Implement the meeting lifecycle and job state machine
      [DESC] Persist lifecycle changes and make each processing step idempotent and safe to retry.
      [DESC] Guard meeting transitions with expected-state revisions and audit them using replay-safe idempotency keys.
      [DESC] Claim processing jobs with expiring lease tokens so stale workers cannot overwrite newer attempts.
      [DESC] Verified schema migration 2 against the live database, nine Rust state-machine tests, database integrity, and warning-free Clippy output.
    $- [ENH] [TI-0025] Separate original evidence from user and model-derived changes
      [DESC] Preserve source segments independently from corrections, translations, and generated interpretations.
      [DESC] Enforce immutable source text and append idempotent, provenance-tagged correction, translation, and romanization revisions.
      [DESC] Verified migration 3 against the live database, source-update rejection, revision selection, idempotent writes, and database integrity.
    $- [ENH] [TI-0026] Define replaceable engine interfaces
      [DESC] Allow engines to change without rewriting stored meeting data.
      [DESC] Provide typed transcription, speaker, and summary contracts with cancellation, progress, retry-aware errors, and replaceable registry entries.
      [DESC] Verified adapter replacement, shared cancellation, progress reporting, typed lookup failures, formatting, and warning-free Clippy output.
    $- [FT] [TI-0027] Support independently downloadable model packs
      [DESC] Keep the core installer small and offer Lite, Balanced, and Accuracy configurations.
      [DESC] Added integrity-checked Fast, Balanced, and Accuracy English packs using base.en, small.en, and medium.en with per-user selection and on-demand installation.

*- [EPIC] [TI-0028] Meeting library and lifecycle management
  [DESC] Provide a clear local workspace for managing meetings throughout their lifecycle.

    $- [FT] [TI-0029] Create, rename, tag, archive, delete, and reopen meetings
      [DESC] Persist retry-safe meeting creation, rename, normalized tags, reversible archive/reopen, and permanent cascade deletion through Tauri commands.
      [DESC] Verified migration 4, lifecycle guards, idempotent retries, nineteen Rust tests, formatting, and warning-free Clippy output.
    $- [FT] [TI-0030] Build the local meeting library
      [DESC] Provide a responsive local library with persisted records, search, status and tag display, archive visibility, and lifecycle actions.
      [DESC] Verified empty and populated states, consented meeting creation, settings interaction, responsive rendering, automated UI tests, and production build.
    $- [FT] [TI-0031] Require consent confirmation before recording
      [DESC] Show an unambiguous recording state and remind users of consent and policy responsibilities.
      [DESC] Block capture until explicit acknowledgement and show distinct live, paused, stopped, and failure states.
    $- [ENH] [TI-0032] Recover an interrupted meeting lifecycle on restart
      [DESC] Reopen unfinished recordings from the last safe checkpoint without corrupting completed work.
      [DESC] Safely stop child capture on shutdown, audit interrupted lifecycle recovery, expose preserved chunks, and allow transcript retry after restart.

*- [EPIC] [TI-0033] Reliable microphone and system-audio capture
  [DESC] Capture meeting audio in recoverable chunks without silently losing data.

    $- [FT] [TI-0034] Select microphone and system-audio sources and capture modes
      [DESC] Discover PulseAudio devices and offer microphone, system, or combined capture with optional WebRTC echo cancellation.
    $- [FT] [TI-0035] Implement start, pause, resume, and safe-stop controls
      [DESC] Enforce a single active recorder, persist lifecycle transitions, restore UI state after reload, and finalize capture safely.
    $- [FT] [TI-0036] Display live audio levels and handle device changes
      [DESC] Publish normalized per-track peaks and surface capture-process or device failure while preserving completed chunks.
    $- [FT] [TI-0037] Write recoverable audio chunks and manifests
      [DESC] Flush progress periodically so forced termination preserves completed chunks.
      [DESC] Integrate atomic session manifests and one-minute PCM WAV chunks into private per-user application storage.
    $- [ENH] [TI-0038] Prevent storage exhaustion during recording
      [DESC] Estimate required space, warn at live thresholds, and stop gracefully when necessary.
      [DESC] Require 512 MB free to start, report live capacity, and finalize safely at the 128 MB reserve.
    #- [FT] [TI-0039] Play back separate captured tracks
      [DESC] The first iteration must capture and safely stop a ten-minute meeting, then play both tracks.
      [DESC] Native testing captured and decoded 31.56 seconds of processed mic, raw mic, and system audio without manifest errors; the required ten-minute endurance run remains.
      [DESC] Native defect evidence (Sep 20, 2026): repeated Play mic/Play system clicks launched seven overlapping ffplay processes with no playing indicator or in-app stop control; playback continued after the desktop shell exited.
      [DESC] Acceptance: own exactly one playback process, make repeat clicks idempotent, replace the active track when another starts, expose meeting/track/elapsed state, and provide an always-visible Stop audio control.
      [DESC] Implemented one backend-owned player, idempotent repeat requests, track replacement, automatic stop before recording or deletion, active button labels, and a persistent meeting/track/time playback bar with Stop audio.
      [DESC] Verified process cleanup against the seven reported players, a managed-child regression test, 30 Rust tests, warning-free Clippy, frontend tests, and the production build; native interaction acceptance remains.
    *- [ENH] [TI-0040] Pass twenty controlled recording scenarios without lost audio
      [DESC] Cover long sessions, silence, disconnects, interruption, low disk space, cross-talk, and poor microphones.
    *- [ENH] [TI-0102] Improve echo cancellation during loud double-talk
      [DESC] Preserve local speech when the user and remote participant speak simultaneously or system playback is loud; address after the working MVP.

